\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Where this survey searched. \emph  {Lower panel:} each searched window is a horizontal segment on its host system's row, rows ordered by distance; heavy blue marks the fine-channel carrier search (Class\nobreakspace  {}A) and light grey the coarse-channel spectral-excess search (Class\nobreakspace  {}B). \emph  {Upper panel:} the number of systems covering each gigahertz of sky frequency. The summed window bandwidth is $23.9\times $ the union, so the union measures frequency coverage and the summed bandwidth search effort.}}{5}{figure.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:waterfall}{{2}{5}{Where this survey searched. \emph {Lower panel:} each searched window is a horizontal segment on its host system's row, rows ordered by distance; heavy blue marks the fine-channel carrier search (Class~A) and light grey the coarse-channel spectral-excess search (Class~B). \emph {Upper panel:} the number of systems covering each gigahertz of sky frequency. The summed window bandwidth is $\GrossOverUnion \times $ the union, so the union measures frequency coverage and the summed bandwidth search effort}{figure.3.2}{}}
\newlabel{eq:accel}{{2}{5}{The de-drifted search statistic}{equation.4.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the 402{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration by Equation\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:accel}, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The right-hand axis is the same quantity as a fractional drift rate, $|\dot  \nu /\nu |=a/c$, which is the unit the literature states drift reach in. The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$, equivalently 12.0\,nHz, which is also the median; it runs to 4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the 5{} TRAPPIST--1{} windows. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the equatorial rotation of a Jupiter, 2.2\,m\,s$^{-2}$ or 7.4\,nHz. Earth's own rotation and orbit, 0.034{} and 0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$, lie below the foot of the axis. \textbf  {Transmitters whose combined orbital and rotational acceleration exceeds the ceiling are not constrained by this search, and the ceiling is a budget they share}: a transmitter on the equator of a rotating giant spends 61{} per cent of it on the planet's spin alone. Nor are unbound transmitters, or any platform accelerating for its own reasons.}}{6}{figure.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{3}{6}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. The drift ceiling of each of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows (points), converted to line-of-sight acceleration by Equation~\ref {eq:accel}, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The right-hand axis is the same quantity as a fractional drift rate, $|\dot \nu /\nu |=a/c$, which is the unit the literature states drift reach in. The boundary drawn is the default ceiling, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$, equivalently \DriftCeilLo \,nHz, which is also the median; it runs to \AccelCeilMax \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the \AccelNWide {} \AccelWideStar {} windows. Horizontal lines are reference accelerations: the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$), and the equatorial rotation of a Jupiter, \AccelJupRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ or \AccelJupRotNHz \,nHz. Earth's own rotation and orbit, \AccelEarthRot {} and \AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$, lie below the foot of the axis. \textbf {Transmitters whose combined orbital and rotational acceleration exceeds the ceiling are not constrained by this search, and the ceiling is a budget they share}: a transmitter on the equator of a rotating giant spends \AccelJupPct {} per cent of it on the planet's spin alone. Nor are unbound transmitters, or any platform accelerating for its own reasons}{figure.4.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}From a crossing to a confirmed signal}{6}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:summary}{{4.2}{6}{From a crossing to a confirmed signal}{subsection.4.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The detection logic, with the count at each level. Three levels and no more: a \emph  {crossing} is an event requiring investigation, an \emph  {unattributed crossing} is a target for follow-up, and a \emph  {confirmed signal} would be a detection. Coincidence with a catalogued transition is a flag and not a disposal, so all 56{} crossings continue past it; the spatial rank and the visibility fit are diagnostics attached to the middle level and decide nothing. The reserved hold-out is drawn as a separate branch because a transmitter in it would matter whatever its calibration role. Every count is read from the ledger, which lists every crossing the search produced, including those in the archival-tail blocks and the quality-failing block that no expectation is computed over, so the figure, Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:ledger} and the text cannot disagree. The counts drawn are the primary crossings, one to a window; the 21{} secondary crossings of \S  \ref  {sec:technosearch} enter the same three levels and three{} reach the second, none the third.}}{6}{figure.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:chain}{{4}{6}{The detection logic, with the count at each level. Three levels and no more: a \emph {crossing} is an event requiring investigation, an \emph {unattributed crossing} is a target for follow-up, and a \emph {confirmed signal} would be a detection. Coincidence with a catalogued transition is a flag and not a disposal, so all \CnCross {} crossings continue past it; the spatial rank and the visibility fit are diagnostics attached to the middle level and decide nothing. The reserved hold-out is drawn as a separate branch because a transmitter in it would matter whatever its calibration role. Every count is read from the ledger, which lists every crossing the search produced, including those in the archival-tail blocks and the quality-failing block that no expectation is computed over, so the figure, Table~\ref {tab:ledger} and the text cannot disagree. The counts drawn are the primary crossings, one to a window; the \CkNSecondary {} secondary crossings of \S \ref {sec:technosearch} enter the same three levels and \CkNSecUnattrWord {} reach the second, none the third}{figure.4.4}{}}
